"""Plain-language explanations shown at the top of every sequence, data-flow and entity page.

Each text answers three things in everyday words: what the user or system is trying to do, what happens step by step,
and the one design decision worth remembering (a race-condition fix or an idempotency guard is named as such).
"""

SEQUENCE_SUMMARIES = {
    '17a-auth-registration-request': 'A new person signs up. The Auth Service creates their profile in the User Service, gives them the default USER role, stores a six-digit code for 15 minutes and asks the Mail Service to send it. If any later step fails, the profile and roles already created are rolled back, so a half-finished account never stays behind (saga compensation).',
    '17b-auth-email-verification': 'The person types the six-digit code from the email. Auth looks the code up in Redis; an unknown or expired code is rejected, a known one marks the profile as verified and the code is deleted so it cannot be used twice. Signing in is a separate step afterwards.',
    '18-auth-login': 'The person signs in with email and password and the app keeps the session. The second part shows what happens on a later cold start: the app tries to restore the saved session and only asks to sign in again when it truly cannot.',
    '19-auth-google': 'The person signs in with Google. Velora checks the Google identity token, then either signs in an existing profile, links the Google identity to a matching one, or creates a new profile. A failed new-profile creation is undone so nothing half-made remains.',
    '20a-auth-refresh-standard': 'The short access token has expired, so the app asks for a new one with its long-lived refresh token. Auth checks the token and replaces it in one atomic database statement: the old token is revoked and a new one created at the same moment. That is why the same token can never be used for two refreshes (race-condition protection).',
    '20b-auth-refresh-deduplication': 'Several requests fail with 401 at once. The app lets only the first one start a refresh and makes the others wait for its result, so one expiry causes one refresh instead of a burst (race-condition protection on the client).',
    '20c-auth-refresh-recovery-theft': 'The refresh response was lost on the way back (bad network). Because the app sends a stable refreshRequestId, Auth recognises the same request inside a short window and hands back the same new token instead of treating it as theft (idempotency). A revoked token used outside that window is treated as replay: all tokens of that user are revoked and the app signs out.',
    '21-auth-authorization': 'One protected request from start to finish: the gateway verifies the token, loads the user and roles (cached 15 minutes in Redis), and only then lets Content serve the Series. Content decides visibility itself for owners and private Reels and asks Friend Service only for other viewers.',
    '22-auth-logout': 'The person logs out while a refresh may be running. The app first blocks new refreshes, waits for the one in flight, asks the backend to revoke the token and only then clears local storage, so a late refresh cannot bring the session back to life.',
    '23a-friend-send-request': 'One person sends a friend request. Friend Service rejects a request to yourself and creates a pending request on a canonical, sorted pair of user ids, so A to B and B to A can never both exist; a unique-conflict is recovered to the existing row. The app then updates its lists and suggestions.',
    '23b-friend-accept-request': 'The recipient accepts a pending request. The update is guarded so only a request that is still pending can be accepted; a second tap or a cancel at the same moment loses cleanly (race-condition protection).',
    '24a-friend-reject-request': 'The recipient declines a pending request. The guarded update only changes a request that is still pending, then the app refreshes its lists.',
    '24b-friend-cancel-request': 'The sender withdraws a request that has not been answered yet. Only the sender can cancel and only while it is still pending.',
    '24c-friend-unfriend': 'One friend removes the other. The friendship row is deleted in one step and both people see the change after the app refreshes its lists.',
    '25-friend-block': 'One person blocks another. Friend Service records the block and removes any friendship or pending request in the same atomic step, then the caches that show friends and suggestions are reconciled so the blocked person disappears everywhere.',
    '26-user-recommendation': 'The app asks for people you may know. User Service builds candidates from the friend graph, falls back to other users when the graph is thin, removes people you already know or blocked, and returns them with the reason each was suggested.',
    '27a-reel-upload-flow': 'The creator publishes a Reel. The app asks for an upload lease and a presigned address, uploads the video straight to Cloudflare R2, and then asks Content to create the Reel. The lease ties the uploaded file to this publish attempt so it cannot be adopted twice.',
    '27b-reel-publish-idempotency': 'The same publish request may arrive twice (a retry, a double tap, two devices). The pair owner plus publishIntentId allows only one Reel; a fingerprint rejects a request that reuses the id with different content, and a race on the database unique rule is reconciled to the Reel that won (idempotency).',
    '28a-media-playback-readiness': 'A media worker picks up the video, makes the streaming version (HLS), uploads it to R2, validates every playlist and segment, and then tells Content the Reel is ready. The Reel becomes playable at this moment; search indexing happens later and never delays playback.',
    '28b-media-evidence-pipeline': 'After the Reel is playable the same worker prepares the evidence search needs: the audio track and sampled keyframes. If that did not finish, Content queues a fallback job about ten minutes later. When the evidence is stored, an index job is handed to the indexing service.',
    '29a-index-intake-routing': 'The indexing service receives a job, claims the attempt so two workers cannot index the same Reel at once, and decides the route: which lane (short or long video) and what work is still needed.',
    '29b-index-multimodal-extraction': 'The indexer turns the evidence into text: it describes keyframes with a vision model, transcribes the audio and asks a model to extract title, tags and a short description. Each finished piece is saved so a retry does not repeat it.',
    '29c-index-vectorization-staging': 'The indexer cuts the text into documents, sections and chunks, checks token limits, reuses vectors it already has and asks the embedding service for the rest. The result is staged as an inactive candidate; nothing searchable changes yet.',
    '29d-index-deferred-visual-retry': 'Some Reels are indexed without visual scenes because the vision quota ran out. Once a day a small batch is re-analysed so these Reels gain visual search when the quota is back.',
    '30a-index-checkpoint-resume': 'If an indexing attempt stops half-way, the same attempt resumes from its saved checkpoints instead of starting over, so finished transcription and embedding work is not paid for twice.',
    '30b-index-failure-retry-dlq': 'What the indexer does with the outcome of a job: acknowledge success, retry later with growing delays, rotate a quota-blocked job behind others, or send a job that cannot succeed to the dead-letter queue where an alert fires.',
    '31a-index-atomic-activation': 'A finished candidate becomes the live index in one step: the old index is switched off and the new one switched on together, the vectors are projected to Qdrant and Content is told. Searchers see either the old index or the new one, never a mix (atomic activation).',
    '31b-index-rollback-recovery': 'Activation can be rejected (a newer attempt already won) or fail half-way. The indexer rolls back the candidate under a per-Reel lock and leaves the previous index untouched, so a late or failed attempt can never damage live search (stale-outcome protection).',
    '32a-feed-cache-paging': 'The app asks for the next page of recommended Reels. Content serves it from a cached feed session, or builds the first page from a shared slate of popular Reels, and starts the personal refill in the background without making the viewer wait.',
    '32b-feed-background-refill': 'In the background Content collects candidates from several pools, ranks them for this viewer, optionally reranks the top ones with a cross-encoder, diversifies them and appends only unseen Reels to the session. A lock stops two refills for the same session running together.',
    '33-cache-invalidation': 'A Reel changes while another request is filling the cache with older data. A version number and a short tombstone make the stale fill lose, so the cache can never overwrite newer data with older data (race-condition protection).',
    '34a-hls-owner-preview': 'Right after publishing, the creator can already watch the original video stored on their own device while the server encodes the streaming version. Other viewers only see a processing placeholder.',
    '34b-hls-validated-stream': 'The app polls the Reel status. When the media is complete and a valid streaming address arrives, the player switches to it and keeps the playback position; if it fails, the app falls back once instead of failing the Reel.',
    '35a-series-create-update': 'A creator creates or edits a Series (a named, ordered group of Reels). Only the owner can change it and a visibility change is applied to its member Reels in the same transaction.',
    '35b-series-membership-order': 'The owner adds, removes or reorders episodes. A serializable transaction re-checks ownership and the exact member set, so two edits at the same time cannot leave duplicate or missing episode numbers (race-condition protection).',
    '36a-series-playback-entry': 'The app opens a Series. Content checks who may see it (owner and private cases locally, friends through Friend Service with a 15-second cache) and returns a window of episodes around the requested one.',
    '36b-series-episode-switch': 'The viewer opens the episode list, picks an episode and scrolls to load more. The list is refreshed only while it is idle, so rows added during a fast scroll cannot make it jump.',
    '37a-share-to-ai-assistant': 'A user shares a Reel with the Velora AI assistant. Content checks the Reel is ready and the sender may share it, records the share and posts a Reel message into the AI conversation. That message alone does not start an AI answer.',
    '37b-share-to-user-chat': 'A user shares a Reel with another person or group. Content asks Friend Service whether sharing is allowed, records the share once (retries reuse it) and Conversation delivers the Reel message to the room.',
    '38a-share-link-create-revoke': 'The owner creates a public link for a Reel or revokes it. A link has a random token and an optional expiry; only the link creator or the Reel owner can revoke it.',
    '38b-share-link-resolution': 'Anyone opens a public link. Content finds the token and only answers when the link is not revoked or expired and the Reel is fully processed; it counts the click and returns minimal Reel details with a ready-to-play stream address. Otherwise it returns an error and counts nothing.',
    '39a-rag-scope-routing': 'A user asks the Velora AI assistant something. The input guardrail runs first, a photo is described, the turn waits for a model slot, the shared Reels the user may ask about are looked up, and the router decides what kind of question it is. Off-topic questions get a short redirect instead of an answer.',
    '39b-rag-search-rerank': 'For questions that need Reel content the assistant plans the search, runs a hybrid search over the index, drops unsafe chunks, reranks with a cross-encoder, adds neighbouring chunks and decides if the evidence is enough. If not, it rewrites the query and tries again once.',
    '40a-rag-answer-memory': 'Before answering, the assistant gets a small window of the chat: the last 12 messages plus up to 80 older ones for recall. It then picks only the memory it needs: the conversation summary and the user\'s saved facts.',
    '40b-rag-turn-memory-update': 'After an answer is saved, a separate event updates memory: every few turns the conversation summary is refreshed, and facts about the user are extracted and stored. Text that fails the memory guardrail is never stored.',
    '41a-rag-draft-verify-cite': 'The assistant drafts an answer from the evidence, a verifier checks every claim against it, and a citation step attaches the supporting Reels. If a check fails the answer is revised or replaced by an honest refusal. After 45 seconds the verifier is skipped to protect response time.',
    '41b-rag-stream-delivery': 'The answer streams to the phone token by token while it is being written, then the final message with citations and recommended Reels is saved and replaces the temporary one. Errors and busy turns are reported as typed failures the app can show.',
    '42a-observability-trace-capture': 'Each AI answer is recorded as a trace in Langfuse: every step, model call and timing. The trace is built around the workflow run so operators can see exactly why an answer was good or bad.',
    '42b-observability-trace-export': 'An operator exports AI traces from Langfuse for review or benchmarks and validates them, so evaluation uses real answers with real evidence.',
    '43a-ai-photo-question': 'A user sends the AI assistant a photo with a caption. The photo is uploaded straight to storage; the AI is only allowed to read photos the sender uploaded themselves, describes it with a vision model, and answers using that description. Quota or unreadable-photo problems come back as clear errors.',
    '43b-ai-guardrails': 'Four safety checks surround one AI turn: the question before any model call, retrieved evidence before it enters the prompt, the answer while it streams, and memory before it is stored. Each check has its own mode (off, shadow or enforce) and always fails open so safety never takes chat down.',
    '43c-ai-conversation-recall': 'For questions like "do you remember what I said about my trip?" the assistant ranks older messages by meaning and words, adds the user\'s saved facts and photo descriptions, and answers from them. If it cannot, it quotes the user\'s own matching message or says honestly it cannot find it.',
    '44a-reel-subtitles': 'When a Reel finishes indexing, its transcript is translated into Vietnamese and English once and stored per language. Viewers then get the stored track instantly; a track made from an old transcript is detected by a hash and translated again.',
}

